---

# 01. Purpose

This document defines how AEGIS will be verified, validated, tested, and accepted throughout its lifecycle.

AEGIS is not an ordinary dashboard.

It is a high-stakes decision-support system operating across:

```text
data
telemetry
intelligence
human decisions
security
operational workflows
```

Therefore, testing must answer more than:

> "Does the application work?"

It must answer:

```text
Does the system behave correctly?

Does the system preserve state correctly?

Does data remain trustworthy?

Does uncertainty remain visible?

Does the AI behave within its boundaries?

Do permissions work correctly?

Does the UI communicate the correct operational state?

Does the system fail safely?

Does the system recover correctly?

Can the complete operational journey be completed?

Can stakeholders trust what the system shows?
```

---

# 02. QA Philosophy

AEGIS follows:

> **Test the system as a system, not merely as code.**

Testing must cover five dimensions:

```text
FUNCTION
DATA
INTELLIGENCE
HUMAN INTERACTION
RESILIENCE
```

---

# 03. Quality Model

AEGIS quality is defined as:

```text
QUALITY
=
Correctness
+
Reliability
+
Security
+
Usability
+
Traceability
+
Resilience
+
Explainability
```

A visually impressive interface that produces incorrect state is not a quality product.

---

# 04. Testing Pyramid

AEGIS should use a layered testing strategy.

```text
                    E2E
                 /       \
          Operational Journey
             /           \
       Integration      Security
          /                 \
      Component          Intelligence
        /                     \
      Unit                  Contract
```

The largest number of tests should be lower in the pyramid.

High-level tests should validate critical journeys rather than replace lower-level tests.

